I sent "build passed" to #general on Slack with one `composio run` script.

**Timestamp of the sent message:** `1767225600.000100`

The command I ran:
```bash
composio run '
  const res = await execute("SLACK_SEND_MESSAGE", { channel: "#general", text: "build passed" });
  const d = res.data ?? {};
  const ts = d.ts ?? d.message?.ts ?? d.response_data?.ts;
  if (!ts) { console.log("No ts found; full response:", JSON.stringify(res, null, 2)); }
  else console.log("Message timestamp:", ts);
'
```

Before running it, I checked the tool's inputs with `composio execute SLACK_SEND_MESSAGE --get-schema`. The only required fields are `channel` and `text`.

The script looks for the timestamp in a few places in the response, because the schema doesn't say where it is. If it doesn't find one, it prints the full response instead.
